This task was already finished before the interruption. Commit 2b0e1afd is in, and the full check (fmt, clippy with -D warnings, build, cargo test) passes on it with a clean working tree. There was nothing left to change, so I made no new commit.

What the commit did:
• The note the model receives. When the search fence withholds lines, the note no longer blames "a ripgrep config via RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH". That stopped being true with yoagent 0.25.5, which passes --no-config to rg and clears RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH and GREP_OPTIONS for both rg and the grep fallback. The note now just says those lines didn't name a file under the searched path, so the fence couldn't check them. It doesn't guess at a cause the code can't know.
• Withholding stays. The fence still drops lines it can't check, and the module doc now lists the shapes that can still produce them:
  • a file name containing a newline (seen happen with GNU grep 3.11);
  • a binary-match notice printed on stdout (GNU grep before 3.5, according to its changelog);
  • an rg or grep wrapper on PATH that adds its own flags;
  • any output the parser doesn't recognise.
  The sentence that depends on yoagent's --no-config carries a yoagent-version-claim: 0.25.5 marker, so the next upgrade has to re-read it.
• Test. A new test checks the exact note text at the point the model receives it, and checks that neither RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH nor "ripgrep config" appears. It failed against the old text before the rewrite. No existing test was edited.
• ARCHITECTURE.md. In the Day-229 entry, the old "--no-config cannot be added" sentence is marked as a superseded claim (true until 0.25.5, b58e4329).
• #1023. The comment is posted with the commit hash, the shapes above, and the two items still open: item 2 (the stale (N matches) footer) and item 3 (find warning lines that pass denied path names).

One false statement is still in the docs: docs/src/configuration/permissions.md lines 212–215 still say a ripgrep config can reshape search results and that unsetting RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH brings dropped matches back. That file was outside this task's scope, so I didn't change it; it's named in both the commit message and the #1023 comment.

